# Semantic Memory

This project is indexed in Postgres and searchable through the `semantic-memory`
MCP server. What is in the index: Markdown (`.md`, `.mdc`, `.mdx`) anywhere in
the repo, GDScript declarations under `src/`, `tools/`, and `test/`, and notes
left by earlier sessions. What is not: `addons/` and other third-party code,
`.tscn`, `.tres`, `project.godot`, and any file over 2 MB. A symbol you cannot
find may simply live outside the index rather than not exist.

The tools describe how to use them; this rule is about when.

When `technical-index-lookup.mdc` also applies, **search wins over reading
`TECHNICAL_INDEX.md` or `IMPLEMENTATION.md` whole**; those files are fallbacks,
not the first open.

## Reading

`DESIGN.md`, `IMPLEMENTATION.md`, and `work-items/` run to hundreds of kilobytes,
so reading one to answer a narrow question spends most of a context window. For
any "how does this work", "why was this decided", or "where is this implemented"
question, call `search_project_memory` first. Don't re-read a file to confirm
prose a result has already shown you — but do open it before you quote a line
number or edit it, because reported line ranges for Markdown can be off by one.

That is a default, not a prohibition. Read the file directly when you are about to
edit it, when you need exact line-by-line control flow, when the file is small
enough that reading it costs no more than a result set, or when it is outside the
index. If two searches come back useless, say so and go read — retrieval lands the
answer in the top five about 88% of the time, so misses are uncommon but real.
Search always returns its full quota of results and never scores them, so judge
relevance yourself rather than assuming the top hit answers the question.

## Writing

Call `sync_project_memory` once you have finished changing documentation or landed
a change in behaviour. It is incremental and takes seconds.

Call `remember` when you work out something a future session would otherwise have
to rediscover: a non-obvious interaction between systems, why an approach was
abandoned, an ordering constraint that cost you time. Notes supplement the
documents rather than replacing them — if the design itself changed, edit
`DESIGN.md` and sync. Re-using a note title overwrites it, so correct a stale note
instead of adding a rival to it.

## If Postgres is unreachable

Say so once, read files directly, and carry on. Do not retry in a loop.
